Body:
@kommander , please check this, I fixed the issue with PR #31322.

Root cause was in packages/tui/src/context/sync.tsx: after the first successful hydration, sync.session.sync() short-circuits via a fullSyncedSessions set, so updates produced by a different writer (another client, remote agent, or opencode serve running the same session) never reach this TUI unless they happen to land on its own SSE stream.

The fix adds an opt-in force?: boolean option to sync.session.sync() that bypasses the once-only cache while still de-duping concurrent in-flight calls, plus a bounded createEffect in the session route that polls sync(id, { force: true }) at a configurable interval (tui.json external_sync_interval_ms, default 2000ms, range 0-60000, 0 disables) only while the session is working or compacting, with onCleanup clearing the interval on route change, idle transition, and unmount.

Hitting this too. For us streaming isn't just unsupported, it corrupts output. Our self-hosted vLLM (Gemma 4 31B) backend has a streaming-only tool-call parser bug that duplicates characters in large tool-call arguments (file contents in write/edit): `<html` → `<<htmlhtml`. The identical request with `stream:false` is clean. So every edit OpenCode makes comes back garbled and it loops re-writing the file.

Setting `options.streaming: false` in the provider config has no effect (v1.15.13). It looks like the field isn't consumed at runtime (related: #5674, and the `buildParams()` hardcoded `stream:true` noted in #24815). Our only workaround is a local proxy that forces `stream:false` upstream and re-serialises the response as SSE.

Requesting a respected per-provider/per-model option to disable streaming, e.g.:

```json
"provider": {
  "vllm": {
    "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
    "options": { "baseURL": "…", "streaming": false },
    "models": { "…": {} }
  }
}
```

so it actually sends `stream:false` to the provider. This unblocks both providers that can't stream and providers whose streaming output is broken.

I managed to get this working with gpt5-4 in azure foundary using this:

`"foundary2": {
      "name": "foundry-gpt",
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "https://foundry-123451.cognitiveservices.azure.com/openai/v1/"
      },
      "models": {
        "gpt-5-4": {
          "name": "gpt-5-4",
          "family": "gpt-5",
          "reasoning": true,
          "interleaved": {
            "field": "reasoning_content"
          },
          "modalities": {
            "input": ["text"],
            "output": ["text"]
          },
          "temperature": true,
          "tool_call": true,
          "attachment": false,
          "experimental": false,
          "status": "active"
        }
      }
    }`
